title: Hierarchical tags
kind: overview
aliases: tag tagging negative_tag mixed_tag vocabulary
summary: Tags are metarecords placed in a tree by their path; a record points at the tags it has, the ones it has not, and — for a folder — the ones it partly has.

A ''tag'' is a metarecord of its own, a ''tag entry'' (`mf_schema = "tag"`), whose
place in the hierarchy is a `tree_ref` field `path`: `musique/jazz` is the entry named
`jazz` under the entry named `musique` ([[TreeRef forest]]). The path is the tag's
identity; an optional `label` may name it for display.

A record says what it is about through three multi-valued `ref` fields pointing at tag
entries:

* `tag` — it ''has'' the tag;
* `negative_tag` — it has ''not'' — an answer, not an absence: a record with neither was never asked;
* `mixed_tag` — a folder whose content is partly tagged and partly not.

Because a tag is an ordinary metarecord, everything else works on it unchanged: `tag ->
(path = "musique/jazz")` finds the records with exactly that tag, `tag -> (path =>*
"musique")` the ones with a music tag of any kind ([[Following references]]), the
simplified language writes them `##=musique/jazz` and `#=musique`
([[Tag syntax in simplified queries]]), and the `treeref` panel browses the vocabulary.

The convention itself — which field is which — lives in the clients, not the daemon:
`mf tag` applies it, with the field names of the `[tag]` table of
[[CLI configuration]], and the GUI's tagging scripts build on `mf tag`.

